          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "There are notable differences between European and US guidelines regarding when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension.\n\n**US Guidelines**:\nIn the United States, stage 1 hypertension is defined as a systolic blood pressure of 130 to 139 mm Hg or diastolic pressure of 80 to 89 mm Hg. According to US guidelines, medication should be initiated alongside lifestyle changes for individuals with stage 1 hypertension if they have established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10% or more. For those without these high-risk features, lifestyle changes are recommended first, with medication considered if blood pressure does not improve after 3 to 6 months of reassessment (source: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\").\n\n**European Guidelines**:\nIn contrast, European guidelines classify hypertension differently, beginning at a threshold of 140/90 mm Hg. Therefore, many individuals that the US classifies as having stage 1 hypertension would not be treated with medication under European criteria, as their blood pressure would be considered high-normal. The European approach generally suggests lifestyle changes alone for patients in the high-normal range, reserving medication for when blood pressure reaches the hypertensive threshold (source: \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\").\n\n**Disagreement Summary**:\n- **US Position**: Starts treatment for stage 1 hypertension (130-139/80-89 mm Hg) with specific high-risk conditions.\n- **European Position**: Defines stage 1 hypertension starting only at 140/90 mm Hg; lower readings are often managed with lifestyle changes alone.\n\nThis difference in defining hypertensive stages leads to a significant variation in which populations receive medication in the two regions, illustrating the need for clarity in guidelines and individualized patient assessment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
